{# ── FAQ ── #}
<section class="section section--cool">
  <div class="container container--narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow">Common Questions</p>
    <h2>The questions I hear from <em>bank staff</em></h2>

    <div style="margin-top: var(--space-5);">
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q" aria-expanded="false" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">Do bank employees get an LMI waiver in Australia?</button>
        <div class="faq-a"><p>Yes, at select lenders. Employees of banks and financial institutions can appear on professional eligibility lists, which allows borrowing at up to 90% LVR with no lenders mortgage insurance. Standard income, credit and loan-size criteria still apply, and the lists change. It is not a guarantee and it is not only a staff perk at your own employer.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q" aria-expanded="false" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">I work in data, digital or tech at a bank, not in lending. Do I still qualify?</button>
        <div class="faq-a"><p>Often, yes. These waivers are generally based on the employer, not the org chart. A data engineer, a digital product manager, a risk officer or a marketing lead can be in the conversation if they are directly employed by an eligible institution. Agency and contractor roles commonly miss out. Confirm your employer against current policy rather than assuming either way.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q" aria-expanded="false" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">Do I have to use my bank's staff package?</button>
        <div class="faq-a"><p>No. Nothing about working for a bank obliges you to borrow from it. The staff package is one option. A broker can put that package next to other lenders, including policies that waive LMI for bank staff more broadly than "only your employer's staff deal." Sometimes the staff deal wins. You should see the comparison.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q" aria-expanded="false" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">How does this compare with the First Home Guarantee?</button>
        <div class="faq-a"><p>The guarantee can work from a 5% deposit with no LMI, but the property has to sit under the Housing Australia cap for that postcode. In Sydney that cap is $1.5 million from 1 October 2025. The occupation waiver typically needs about 10% and does not use a government price cap, though lender limits still apply. Which path wins depends on the suburb, the deposit, and whether you are a first home buyer.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q" aria-expanded="false" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">What happens to the loan if I leave the bank later?</button>
        <div class="faq-a"><p>On the waiver side, nothing. LMI is a one-off premium dealt with at settlement, so a loan written with the waiver does not acquire a premium afterwards. That differs from staff pricing at your own employer, which typically ends with your employment. Apply while eligibility exists. Do not rush a purchase that is not ready.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q" aria-expanded="false" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">How do bonuses and RSUs affect a bank-staff home loan?</button>
        <div class="faq-a"><p>The waiver is about LMI. Your borrowing capacity is about how a lender counts the package. Bonus and RSU income can be counted, shaded, or ignored depending on the lender, the history, and the paperwork. That gap can dwarf the LMI saving. I walked through it here: what your RSUs are worth to a bank.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-q" aria-expanded="false" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">Does using a broker cost bank staff anything?</button>
        <div class="faq-a"><p>No. Brokers are paid by the lender at settlement, and under the Best Interests Duty I am legally required to act in your interests. If the staff package is the better deal, that is what I should tell you. Twenty minutes on a call and you will know what you qualify for.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>

