      "name": "Do bank employees get an LMI waiver in Australia?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes, at select lenders. Employees of banks and financial institutions can appear on professional eligibility lists, which allows borrowing at up to 90% LVR with no lenders mortgage insurance. Standard income, credit and loan-size criteria still apply, and the lists change. It is not a guarantee and it is not only a staff perk at your own employer."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "I work in data, digital or tech at a bank, not in lending. Do I still qualify?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Often, yes. These waivers are generally based on the employer, not the org chart. A data engineer, a digital product manager, a risk officer or a marketing lead can be in the conversation if they are directly employed by an eligible institution. Agency and contractor roles commonly miss out. Confirm your employer against current policy rather than assuming either way."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Do I have to use my bank's staff package?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "No. Nothing about working for a bank obliges you to borrow from it. The staff package is one option. A broker can put that package next to other lenders, including policies that waive LMI for bank staff more broadly than only your employer's staff deal. Sometimes the staff deal wins. You should see the comparison."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How does this compare with the First Home Guarantee?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The guarantee can work from a 5% deposit with no LMI, but the property has to sit under the Housing Australia cap for that postcode. In Sydney that cap is $1.5 million from 1 October 2025. The occupation waiver typically needs about 10% and does not use a government price cap, though lender limits still apply. Which path wins depends on the suburb, the deposit, and whether you are a first home buyer."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What happens to the loan if I leave the bank later?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "On the waiver side, nothing. LMI is a one-off premium dealt with at settlement, so a loan written with the waiver does not acquire a premium afterwards. That differs from staff pricing at your own employer, which typically ends with your employment. Apply while eligibility exists. Do not rush a purchase that is not ready."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How do bonuses and RSUs affect a bank-staff home loan?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The waiver is about LMI. Your borrowing capacity is about how a lender counts the package. Bonus and RSU income can be counted, shaded, or ignored depending on the lender, the history, and the paperwork. That gap can dwarf the LMI saving. I walked through it here: what your RSUs are worth to a bank."
      }
